1
00:00:06,640 --> 00:00:17,090
Minnesota, you chose to put your trust in me.
明尼苏达州的人民，你们选择把信任交给我。

2
00:00:20,800 --> 00:00:25,360
A campaign built on substance and not divisiveness.
这场竞选着眼于实质问题，拒绝制造分裂。

3
00:00:25,900 --> 00:00:26,720
On hope,
选择希望，

4
00:00:26,880 --> 00:00:29,320
Hope and not on hate.
选择希望，而非仇恨。

5
00:00:29,900 --> 00:00:33,380
And it is time to move this state forward.
现在，是时候推动本州向前发展了。

6
00:00:34,155 --> 00:00:42,780
I am not interested in moving Minnesota to the left or the right.
我对将明尼苏达州向左或向右移动不感兴趣。

7
00:00:42,780 --> 00:00:44,400
I want to move us forward.
我想推动我们前进。

8
00:00:44,614 --> 00:00:54,450
I am going to be working not just for everyone who voted for me but for all Minnesotans.
我将为所有明尼苏达州民众工作，包括每一位投票给我的人。

9
00:00:58,710 --> 00:01:04,570
Whether or not I got your vote, I will give you my best.
无论我是否得到你的投票，我都会给你最好的。

10
00:01:06,250 --> 00:01:21,380
I want to thank my family, John, our daughter Abigail, my lieutenant governor-elect Ben [surname unclear] and his family,
我要感谢我的家人、约翰、女儿阿比盖尔，以及候任副州长本[姓氏不清]和他的家人，

11
00:01:21,760 --> 00:01:27,220
His wife Tessa, who you're about to see, his five kids, his two cats,
他的妻子泰莎，你即将见到的，他的五个孩子，他的两只猫，

12
00:01:27,220 --> 00:01:28,600
And his one dog.
还有他的一只狗。

13
00:01:29,320 --> 00:01:34,200
I want to thank my incredible staff on this campaign.
我要感谢这次活动中出色的工作人员。

14
00:01:35,340 --> 00:01:38,540
I want to thank our Senate office.
我要感谢我们的参议院办公室。

15
00:01:38,960 --> 00:01:44,766
And I also, now listen, I want to thank my opponent, Lisa Damoth,
现在我还要感谢我的对手丽莎·达莫斯，

16
00:01:44,780 --> 00:01:49,520
Who just called to congratulate me, which I truly appreciate.
谁刚刚打电话祝贺我，我真的很感激。

17
00:01:49,520 --> 00:01:58,680
And I want to wish her family and Lisa the best.
我祝愿她的家人和丽莎一切顺利。

18
00:01:58,840 --> 00:02:02,620
And thank her for her work with the House of Representatives.
感谢她与众议院的合作。

19
00:02:03,140 --> 00:02:11,600
I want to thank this incredible ticket which is going to win Amy to Zach a to Z.
我要感谢这张令人难以置信的彩票，它将赢得艾米到扎克的所有奖项。

20
00:02:11,980 --> 00:02:19,690
And to the people of Minnesota, thank you.
感谢明尼苏达州人民。

21
00:02:23,270 --> 00:02:26,990
Make no mistake, this was a hard election.
毫无疑问，这是一场艰难的选举。

22
00:02:27,350 --> 00:02:35,310
Outside money flooding our state, ugly and hateful attacks that were meant to divide us.
外部资金涌入我们的州，丑陋和仇恨的攻击旨在分裂我们。

23
00:02:35,810 --> 00:02:38,710
But I think we learned a few things tonight.
但我认为今晚我们学到了一些东西。

24
00:02:39,390 --> 00:02:45,810
For one, we learned that a Packers ca fan cannot buy our governor's seat.
首先，我们了解到包装工队的球迷无法购买我们的州长席位。

25
00:02:46,010 --> 00:02:52,440
Even if that guy is a billionaire.
即使那家伙是亿万富翁。

26
00:02:53,760 --> 00:03:00,540
We learned that dark money doesn't win even when we were outspent two to one on ads.
我们了解到，即使我们在广告上的支出比我们多出两比一，黑钱也不会获胜。

27
00:03:01,360 --> 00:03:07,260
And we learned that you can't buy a seat when the people of Minnesota vote.
我们了解到，当明尼苏达州人民投票时，你买不到席位。

28
00:03:07,760 --> 00:03:14,240
And when the people of Minnesota show up and you showed up, Minnesota,
当明尼苏达州人民出现并且你出现时，明尼苏达州，

29
00:03:15,140 --> 00:03:17,760
You showed up like you have all year.
你像一整年一样出现了。

30
00:03:17,960 --> 00:03:19,320
You inspire me.
你激励我。

31
00:03:19,320 --> 00:03:23,700
And as your next governor, I will work my heart out for you.
作为你们的下一任州长，我将竭尽全力为你们服务。

32
00:03:24,240 --> 00:03:30,160
I promise that I will be a transformative governor for this state.
我保证我将成为本州变革性的州长。

33
00:03:31,662 --> 00:03:39,140
In Minnesota, trust is not given, it is earned.
在明尼苏达州，信任不是给予的，而是赢得的。

34
00:03:39,780 --> 00:03:43,320
It comes with expectations and that is results.
它伴随着期望，那就是结果。

35
00:03:44,040 --> 00:03:45,940
And we will deliver results.
我们将交付成果。

36
00:03:46,040 --> 00:03:53,560
Results that you can see and feel in your life and we will do it with our legislature.
您在生活中可以看到和感受到的结果，我们将与我们的立法机关一起做到这一点。

37
00:03:58,320 --> 00:04:05,060
So you deserve to afford a good life, buy that first home, start that business, Retire with dignity.
所以你应该负担得起美好的生活，购买第一套房子，开始创业，有尊严地退休。

38
00:04:05,820 --> 00:04:09,980
You deserve a state government that matches your drive and ambition.
您值得拥有一个与您的动力和抱负相匹配的州政府。

39
00:04:10,500 --> 00:04:12,240
And that is what we're going to do.
这就是我们要做的。

40
00:04:12,980 --> 00:04:18,680
We will root out the waste, the fraud, and the abuse and cut through the red tape.
我们将根除浪费、欺诈和滥用行为，并消除繁文缛节。

41
00:04:18,680 --> 00:04:23,640
A top tobottom audit of state government that starts on day one.
从第一天开始对州政府进行自上而下的审计。

42
00:04:23,920 --> 00:04:26,240
If something works, we will keep it.
如果有东西有效，我们就会保留它。

43
00:04:26,300 --> 00:04:29,840
If it's broken, we will fix it or get rid of it.
如果它坏了，我们将修复它或摆脱它。

44
00:04:30,100 --> 00:04:39,300
We are going to speed up government, rebuild trust, and work for you.
我们将加快政府进程，重建信任，并为您服务。

45
00:04:40,020 --> 00:04:48,560
And I'm looking forward to bringing in a new team and working together with our state employees to make that happen.
我期待着引入一个新的团队，并与我们的州雇员一起努力实现这一目标。

46
00:04:49,608 --> 00:04:54,640
This is not going to be easy to do.
这并不容易做到。

47
00:04:55,320 --> 00:05:00,180
I will need people out there at my side, both parties.
我需要有人在我身边，双方。

48
00:05:00,640 --> 00:05:06,880
And spoiler alert, you may not like every single thing I do, but that is what I ran on.
剧透警告，你可能不喜欢我做的每一件事，但这就是我所做的。

49
00:05:07,160 --> 00:05:08,660
And that is getting things done.
这就是把事情做好。

50
00:05:09,240 --> 00:05:11,480
So we face headwinds every day.
所以我们每天都面临逆风。

51
00:05:12,500 --> 00:05:17,720
Skyrocketing gas prices due to Trump's war, rising health care costs and his tariffs.
由于特朗普的战争、不断上涨的医疗费用和他的关税，天然气价格飞涨。

52
00:05:17,720 --> 00:05:24,420
But as you know right now, he has had a lot of rubber stance to enable him in these last two years.
但正如你现在所知，在过去的两年里，他有很多橡胶立场来支持他。

53
00:05:24,880 --> 00:05:28,005
But tonight, the ink ran out.
但今晚，墨水用完了。

54
00:05:28,005 --> 00:05:37,990
It ran out in Texas.
它在德克萨斯州用完了。

55
00:05:39,040 --> 00:05:45,326
It It ran out in Ohio.
它在俄亥俄州用完了。

56
00:05:46,593 --> 00:05:51,823
It It ran out in South Carolina.
它在南卡罗来纳州用完了。

57
00:05:51,833 --> 00:06:04,269
And it ran out because next senator from the state of Minnesota will be our own Peggy Flanigan.
它已经用完了，因为明尼苏达州的下一位参议员将是我们自己的佩吉·弗拉尼根。

58
00:06:12,720 --> 00:06:32,850
So, so Minnesota, I'm not going to be a rubber stamp for Donald Trump and I will do my job without fear or favor.
所以，明尼苏达州，我不会成为唐纳德·特朗普的橡皮图章，我将毫无恐惧或偏袒地完成我的工作。

59
00:06:33,850 --> 00:06:38,150
I will govern for motans and nobody else.
我将为莫坦斯而不是其他人执政。

60
00:06:38,750 --> 00:06:44,910
And while the headwinds we face may well be a constant, they can never be an excuse.
尽管我们面临的阻力很可能是持续存在的，但它们永远不能成为借口。

61
00:06:45,710 --> 00:06:53,070
That's why I've committed to bring down the cost of health care premiums and bring down the cost of prescription drugs.
这就是为什么我致力于降低医疗保健费用和处方药的成本。

62
00:06:54,030 --> 00:07:02,570
Working working with the private sector to build 100,000 new homes from apartments to single family homes.
与私营部门合作建造 100,000 套新住宅，从公寓到单户住宅。

63
00:07:02,830 --> 00:07:14,150
We are going to have a growth mindset and we're going to have a growth agenda because we must continue to be a state
我们将拥有增长心态，我们将制定增长议程，因为我们必须继续成为一个国家

64
00:07:14,150 --> 00:07:18,630
That makes stuff that invents things and exports to the world.
它制造出的东西可以发明东西并出口到世界各地。

65
00:07:19,050 --> 00:07:27,350
And we are going to make it easier to start a business and double the number of apprenticeships.
我们将使创业变得更容易，并将学徒数量增加一倍。

66
00:07:30,280 --> 00:07:37,900
And when it comes to our schools, we're going to see student achievement up and screen time in the classroom down.
对于我们的学校来说，我们将看到学生成绩的提高和课堂上的屏幕时间的减少。

67
00:07:41,980 --> 00:07:49,300
So after Tim decided not to run, and by the way, we thank Tim and Gwen for their commitment to our state.
因此，在蒂姆决定不参选之后，顺便说一句，我们感谢蒂姆和格温对我们州的承诺。

68
00:07:50,583 --> 00:07:59,500
I look back at the year Minnesota had losing Melissa and Mark, the shooting of the Hoffman's,
我回顾明尼苏达州失去梅丽莎和马克的那一年，霍夫曼一家的枪击事件，

69
00:07:59,940 --> 00:08:06,480
The kids in the church, the 3,000 ICE agents, the serious fraud problem,
教堂里的孩子、3000 名 ICE 特工、严重的欺诈问题、

70
00:08:06,680 --> 00:08:09,520
And the affront on trust in our government.
以及对我们政府信任的侮辱。

71
00:08:10,120 --> 00:08:15,480
You know, I've loved my job in the Senate, but I love our state more.
你知道，我很喜欢我在参议院的工作，但我更爱我们的州。

72
00:08:16,079 --> 00:08:27,950
In the words of Harry Truman about his years in Washington, I tried to never forget who I was,
用哈里·杜鲁门 (Harry Truman) 谈到他在华盛顿的岁月时的话来说，我努力永远不要忘记自己是谁，

73
00:08:28,230 --> 00:08:32,110
Where I came from, and where I would go back to.
我从哪里来，又要回到哪里。

74
00:08:32,850 --> 00:08:34,270
I know where I come from.
我知道我从哪里来。

75
00:08:34,670 --> 00:08:38,010
I know who I am, and I know who I ran for.
我知道我是谁，我也知道我为谁而竞选。

76
00:08:38,390 --> 00:08:40,150
The people of Minnesota.
明尼苏达州人民。

77
00:08:40,825 --> 00:08:51,390
People across each of our 87 incredible counties that I travel to every year.
我每年都会去 87 个令人难以置信的县，那里的人们遍布其中。

78
00:08:51,790 --> 00:08:56,010
I want to thank our staff because we walked 40 miles of parades.
我要感谢我们的工作人员，因为我们走了 40 英里的游行。

79
00:08:56,590 --> 00:09:00,490
We had hours lines outside our state fair booth.
我们在州博览会展位外排了几个小时的队。

80
00:09:01,170 --> 00:09:04,599
We did 30,000 launch signs thanks to you.
感谢您，我们制作了 30,000 个发射标志。

81
00:09:06,960 --> 00:09:12,257
I think of the people we met, the Nelson family outside of Morehead.
我想起了我们遇到的人，莫尔黑德郊外的纳尔逊一家。

82
00:09:12,280 --> 00:09:15,360
Four generations who have now farmed the land.
现在已经有四代人耕种这片土地了。

83
00:09:15,800 --> 00:09:18,480
Kate Lungquist, a breast cancer survivor.
凯特·朗奎斯特，乳腺癌幸存者。

84
00:09:19,040 --> 00:09:30,880
Julie Pider, her turn who turned her grief into a mission and carried it all the way to Washington after losing her husband to Cancer from toxins.
朱莉·皮德 (Julie Pider) 在失去丈夫后，将悲伤转化为使命，一路来到华盛顿。癌症来自毒素。

85
00:09:32,080 --> 00:09:35,470
The students at Apollo High School in St. Cloud.
圣克劳德阿波罗高中的学生。

86
00:09:35,470 --> 00:09:40,180
Gwen Elliott, who lives in a manufactured home in Blaine and has fought,
格温·埃利奥特 (Gwen Elliott) 住在布莱恩的活动房屋里，曾与

87
00:09:40,180 --> 00:09:45,860
As we will do, with the legislature, to make sure these homeowners are treated fairly.
正如我们将与立法机关所做的那样，以确保这些房主受到公平对待。

88
00:09:47,800 --> 00:09:55,740
And I think my friends of Harper Mo's parents, listen to this, Mike and Jackie,
我想哈珀·莫父母的朋友们，听听这个，迈克和杰基，

89
00:09:55,844 --> 00:10:03,900
Little Harper, who had just begun fifth grade when those bullets rained through the stained glass windows.
当子弹从彩色玻璃窗中倾泻而下时，小哈珀刚刚上五年级。

90
00:10:04,680 --> 00:10:06,880
She would have turned 12 today.
今天她就满12岁了。

91
00:10:07,380 --> 00:10:10,100
Today is her birthday.
今天是她的生日。

92
00:10:11,960 --> 00:10:18,180
So, thanks to your work and what I think is happening with the Minnesota legislature today,
因此，感谢您的工作以及我认为明尼苏达州立法机关今天所发生的事情，

93
00:10:18,998 --> 00:10:32,070
We will get that gun safety vote and I will sign that bill into law.
我们将进行枪支安全投票，我将将该法案签署为法律。

94
00:10:32,100 --> 00:10:34,920
I think of so many of you that have stood up.
我想到你们中有很多人站起来了。

95
00:10:35,680 --> 00:10:41,580
When ice descended on our state, you brought food to strangers and kids to school.
当冰雪降临我们州时，你们为陌生人和孩子们送去学校的食物。

96
00:10:42,220 --> 00:10:51,480
You marched 50,000 strong in sub-zero weather in what will be one of the top civil rights moments of our century in this country.
你们在零度以下的天气里游行了五万人，这将是这个国家本世纪最重要的民权时刻之一。

97
00:10:51,827 --> 00:10:59,840
And after the horror of Renee Good and Alex Prey, their senseless killings,
在蕾妮·古德和亚历克斯·普雷的恐怖经历以及他们毫无意义的杀戮之后，

98
00:11:00,220 --> 00:11:01,720
We didn't look down.
我们没有往下看。

99
00:11:02,220 --> 00:11:03,100
We looked up.
我们抬头一看。

100
00:11:03,780 --> 00:11:05,140
We didn't look away.
我们没有移开目光。

101
00:11:05,560 --> 00:11:06,860
We looked at each other.
我们面面相觑。

102
00:11:07,660 --> 00:11:14,264
And with their family's guidance, I went back to Washington and grilled Christy Gnome.
在他们家人的指导下，我回到华盛顿，烤了克里斯蒂·诺姆。

103
00:11:14,264 --> 00:11:15,660
And the next day, she got fired.
第二天，她就被解雇了。

104
00:11:18,027 --> 00:11:27,750
And months after that, months after that, I got a call from Ambassador Caroline Kennedy.
几个月后，几个月后，我接到了卡罗琳·肯尼迪大使的电话。

105
00:11:28,210 --> 00:11:32,910
She said they're giving the profiling courage award named after her dad to Minnesota.
她说他们将以她父亲的名字命名的勇气奖颁给明尼苏达州。

106
00:11:33,208 --> 00:11:37,710
And she asked me, she asked me, should it go to an elected official?
她问我，她问我，它应该交给民选官员吗？

107
00:11:38,510 --> 00:11:40,390
And I said, "No, give it to the people."
我说：“不，把它交给人民。”

108
00:11:41,110 --> 00:11:42,470
And that is what happened.
这就是发生的事情。

109
00:11:42,530 --> 00:11:48,110
And a few weeks later, a middle school class from St. Paul came to see me.
几周后，圣保罗的一个中学班来找我。

110
00:11:48,230 --> 00:11:54,590
And this little 14-year-old M girl who was this tall, but she stood like she was six feet tall.
还有这个14岁的小M女孩，这么高，但站起来就像有六英尺高。

111
00:11:55,190 --> 00:11:59,410
She looked at me and said, "So the people got the award?" I said, "Yeah."
她看着我说：“所以这些人获奖了？”我说：“是啊。”

112
00:11:59,970 --> 00:12:04,250
And then she said, "That means she said I got the award."
然后她说：“这意味着她说我获奖了。”

113
00:12:04,590 --> 00:12:06,690
She said, "I got the award."
她说：“我获奖了。”

114
00:12:07,360 --> 00:12:12,210
That is the spirit with which I decided to take this job.
这就是我决定接受这份工作的精神。

115
00:12:12,390 --> 00:12:15,370
And that is a spirit of our state.
这就是我们国家的精神。

116
00:12:15,830 --> 00:12:19,990
It is a spirit that will carry us to a higher ground.
这种精神将带领我们走向更高的境界。

117
00:12:20,270 --> 00:12:22,970
We will not rest on our laurels.
我们不会固步自封。

118
00:12:23,450 --> 00:12:25,430
And I will end with my story.
我将以我的故事结束。

119
00:12:26,010 --> 00:12:30,530
My grandpa worked 1500 ft underground up in northern Minnesota.
我的祖父在明尼苏达州北部 1500 英尺的地下工作。

120
00:12:30,530 --> 00:12:37,310
He saved money in a coffee can in the basement to send my dad to a two-year community college.
他在地下室的一个咖啡罐里存了钱，送我父亲去一所两年制社区大学。

121
00:12:37,770 --> 00:12:44,530
My dad went on from that hard scrabble mining town to be a beloved journalist covering the Minnesota Vikings.
我父亲从那个艰苦的拼字游戏小镇长大后，成为一名报道明尼苏达维京人队的受人爱戴的记者。

122
00:12:45,210 --> 00:12:51,410
My mom, a proud union member, and thank you labor for being in the house.
我的妈妈，一位自豪的工会成员，感谢你们来到家里。

123
00:12:53,658 --> 00:13:01,370
My mom taught second grade until she was 70 years old.
我妈妈教二年级直到她70岁。

124
00:13:01,370 --> 00:13:06,270
I still have people come up to me on the street and say she is their favorite teacher.
仍然有人在街上来找我，说她是他们最喜欢的老师。

125
00:13:06,730 --> 00:13:13,810
So I stand before you today as a granddaughter of an iron or minor who never got to graduate from high school,
所以今天我作为一个从未高中毕业的铁人或未成年人的孙女站在你们面前，

126
00:13:14,190 --> 00:13:17,030
The daughter of a teacher and a newspaper man.
教师和报人的女儿。

127
00:13:17,450 --> 00:13:26,860
And thanks to you, the first woman elected to the United States Senate from the state of Minnesota.
感谢您，第一位从明尼苏达州当选为美国参议员的女性。

128
00:13:27,140 --> 00:13:33,200
And thanks to you, the first woman elected governor of the state of Minnesota.
感谢您，第一位当选明尼苏达州州长的女性。

129
00:13:33,200 --> 00:13:46,060
Our past, Minnesota, has made us who we are, but our future is up to us.
明尼苏达州，我们的过去造就了现在的我们，但我们的未来取决于我们。

130
00:13:46,640 --> 00:13:48,900
I want to build that future together.
我想共同建设这个未来。

131
00:13:49,500 --> 00:13:53,940
I want to build that future for everyone, and I know you do, Too.
我想为每个人建设这样的未来，我知道你也这么做，也是。

132
00:13:54,340 --> 00:13:56,060
We have a lot of work to do.
我们还有很多工作要做。

133
00:13:56,540 --> 00:14:03,300
We're going to start right away working with our legislators, Aaron Murphy, Zack Stevenson, The next leader.
我们将立即开始与我们的立法者亚伦·墨菲、扎克·史蒂文森合作，下一个领导者。

134
00:14:05,490 --> 00:14:06,762
Peggy Flanigan.
佩吉·弗拉尼根。

135
00:14:06,762 --> 00:14:20,110
And by the way, let's let's thank Tina Smith for her service with Keith Ellison and Steve Simon.
顺便说一句，让我们感谢蒂娜·史密斯为基思·埃里森和史蒂夫·西蒙提供的服务。

136
00:14:22,879 --> 00:14:29,670
And Zack Filipovich and when we're done, everyone will know how to say his name.
还有扎克·菲利波维奇（Zack Filipovich），当我们完成后，每个人都会知道如何说出他的名字。

137
00:14:30,530 --> 00:14:44,070
And Ben Sher, Minnesota's next lieutenant governor, the first person, the first person elected statewide from the seventh congressional district in over 90 years.
还有明尼苏达州的下一任副州长本·谢尔（Ben Sher），他是 90 多年来第一人，第一个从第七个国会选区在全州范围内当选的人。

138
00:14:44,650 --> 00:14:47,230
So, let us get to work.
那么，让我们开始工作吧。

139
00:14:47,750 --> 00:14:49,090
Let us move forward.
让我们继续前进。

140
00:14:49,410 --> 00:14:52,970
And tonight, we will celebrate this election.
今晚，我们将庆祝这次选举。

141
00:14:53,310 --> 00:14:53,920
Thank you everyone.
谢谢大家。
